SUMMARY:
# Summary of `main::get_path` Disassembly (0x1fcd0–0x20110)

## Function Overview
`_ZN4main8get_path17h8908b1fe749b5357E` is a Rust function `main::get_path` that:
1. Prints a prompt (via `std::io::stdio::print`).
2. Flushes stdout.
3. Reads a line from stdin into a `String`.
4. Strips trailing newline characters (`\n` = 0xa and `\r` = 0xd) from the end of the input.
5. Returns the resulting `String` (moved into the caller-provided return slot at `-0x138(%rbp)`).

## Key Call Sequence
- `0x1fcf6`: `alloc::string::String::new` — creates empty String at `-0x130(%rbp)`.
- `0x1fd1c`: `core::fmt::Arguments::new_v1` — builds format args using static fmt string at `0x286dd0` and `str.t` at `0x670d0`.
- `0x1fd2a`: `std::io::stdio::print` — prints the prompt.
- `0x1fd53`: `std::io::stdio::stdout` — gets stdout handle.
- `0x1fd87`: `Stdout::flush` — flushes stdout.
- `0x1fdbc`: `std::io::stdio::stdin` — gets stdin handle.
- `0x1fdf5`: `Stdin::read_line` — reads a line into the String at `-0x130(%rbp)`.
- `0x1fe16`: `Result::expect` — unwraps read result (panics on error).
- `0x1fe4e`: `String::deref` — gets `&str` view of the input.
- `0x1fe78`: `str::chars` — creates a `Chars` iterator.
- `0x1fe8b`: `Chars::next_back` — gets last char.
- `0x1fed3`: `String::pop` — pops last char if it is `\n` (0xa).
- `0x1fefd`: `String::deref` again.
- `0x1ff41`: `str::chars` again.
- `0x1ff51`: `Chars::next_back` again.
- `0x1ff93`: `String::pop` — pops last char if it is `\r` (0xd).
- `0x1ffb3`–`0x1fff2`: Copies the 3-word String struct (ptr, cap, len) into the return slot at `-0x138(%rbp)`.

## Newline-Stripping Logic (important)
Two separate checks, each using `next_back` + `sub` + `je`:
- First check at `0x1feba`: `sub $0x1, %edx` then `je 0x1fee3`; at `0x1fee3`: `mov -0x9c(%rbp),%eax; sub $0xa,%eax; je 0x1fecc` → pops if last char == `\n`.
